{"uncertainty": 0.2, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "uart_tx regression failure: start pulse during a frame overwrites the transmit holding register", "summary": "Requirement [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. Inputs: the approved RTL [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1] (review [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.e1]), the VeriTriage session [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e1] / [run:run-0001], and the Icarus reproduction [evidence:prj-a0b3e1ca75.triage.e2] / [run:run-0002]. Observation: the failure is deterministic. The build succeeded, $finish was reached at 546000, and 1 of 4 checks failed [run:run-0002]. VeriTriage gave only 30% confidence and the class unknown_failure [run:run-0001], so I treated its 'Likely RTL bug' hypothesis as one candidate, not as the conclusion.\n\nDEFECT (rank 1, concluded: rtl_bug). In [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1] the always block runs `if (start) hold <= data;` before the case statement, in every state. The IDLE branch already loads hold, so this statement only changes behaviour when state is START, DATA or STOP. The DATA branch reads the transmitted bits back from `hold[bit_idx + 3'd1]`.\n\nCausality: a start pulse that arrives while busy is high rewrites hold. Every data bit loaded after that clock edge comes from the new byte, so the in-flight frame goes out corrupted. Its framing and timing stay intact, which is consistent with a single failed data check among 4.\n\nIncorrectness: the module's own header contract says 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.' The design ignores the pulse for the state machine, which stays in its current state, but not for the data. The defect is the write to hold, and it happens cycles before the failing check reads tx.\n\nSuggested fix, not applied here: delete the top-level `if (start) hold <= data;` and leave the load in IDLE.\nRefuting evidence: the repro below passes on this RTL, or it also fails on the clean uart_tx.\n\nALTERNATIVES CONSIDERED:\n(2) testbench_bug, rejected. I derived the expected tx bits again from the header contract (8N1, LSB first, a mid-frame start is ignored), not from the RTL's output, and the RTL path above explains the mismatch on its own. Refuting evidence: the same specification-derived repro fails on the clean RTL.\n(3) infrastructure, rejected. The run built, reached $finish and produced a verdict with a specific check count [run:run-0002]. That does not look like a tool or flow crash. Refuting evidence: a clean rebuild with no other change gives a different result.\n(4) spec_ambiguity, rejected. The clause the check relies on, the header sentence on start while busy, fixes the behaviour explicitly. Refuting evidence: a governing specification outside this artifact that allows a reload while busy. None is in my inputs."}], "files": [{"path": "tb/uart_tx_root_cause_repro.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Minimal self-checking repro: start pulse during data bit 2 must not change the frame in flight", "summary": "Repro for [requirement:req-d10c4df532] against [artifact:prj-a0b3e1ca75.design-under-investigation.a1]. Setup: CLKS_PER_BIT=4, synchronous reset, one frame of 0xA5. During data bit 2 the testbench pulses start for one cycle with 0x5A, which has every bit different from 0xA5.\n\nChecks (13), all taken from the header contract and none from the RTL's internal names:\n- idle after reset\n- the start bit, the 8 data bits LSB-first and the stop bit, each sampled mid-bit relative to the accepted start pulse\n- busy is high when the intruding pulse arrives\n- the line stays idle afterwards, so no second frame starts\n\nPredicted, not run: on this RTL data bits 3..7 come out of 0x5A, so 5 checks fail and the test reproduces the failure seen in [run:run-0002]. On RTL that ignores the pulse, all checks pass. The testbench prints exactly one NIRMAAN-SIM verdict line and then calls $finish.", "entry": "uart_tx_root